GNU toolchain path
==================

`make TOOLCHAIN=gnu` (or plain `make` on Linux) builds reset.bin and q.bin with
sparc64-linux-gnu-{gcc,as,ld,objcopy}. No Sun software.

Files
-----
- `hv-opts.sh` -- prints the -D/-U set straight out of Sun's makefiles, never
  hand-copied.
- `mkoverrides.py` -- shadow copies of the few headers whose forward-use
  declarations ISO C rejects, so gcc accepts them.
- `../dwarf2genassym.pl` -- generates offsets.h without Sun's `stabs`: gcc
  writes DWARF for a stub that refers to every type offsets.in names, and the
  script reads the layout out of `readelf --debug-dump=info`, with the
  semantics of `stabs -t genassym`. The same script serves the Sun path, where
  Developer Studio 12.6 cannot write stabs either (it reads `dwarfdump` there).
- (`genoffsets.py`, the earlier technique -- inline-asm markers whose operands
  are compile-time constants -- now lives in tests/full/hv_reset/lib/ and is
  the second, independent computation in the offsets-cross-check test.)
- `pushlocals.py` -- reimplements Sun's `.pushlocals`/`.poplocals` for GNU as,
  which has no equivalent.
- `build-offsets.sh` -- runs the above and checks the result against
  `reference/offsets.ontario-legion.h`.
- `build.sh` -- the full build: source fixups for constructs GNU as rejects,
  assemble, compile, link with a from-scratch q.ld, then reset.bin.
- `reference/offsets.ontario-legion.h` -- a real offsets.h generated by Sun's
  `stabs` for this exact target. The check oracle only: with the default
  generator the output is byte-identical to it and nothing is copied from it.

Confirmed
---------
Builds clean from the unmodified src/hypervisor, and the resulting binaries
boot to the same OBP banner the all-Sun-toolchain build produces.

The only external dependency beyond src/hypervisor is sys/stack.h, vendored at
abi/sys/stack.h (abi/README).

Byte identity with the sun build: not a goal
--------------------------------------------
The two toolchains will never produce identical bytes. Different assemblers
encode the same constants differently, different compilers generate different
code, and the linker scripts differ. (Sun cc's debug stubs, which once added
the build directory to q.bin, are removed from the objects on the sun path
now: build/hv_reset/README.)

reset.bin has the same size on both paths, 768 bytes: the GNU build passes
--no-pad-sections to as. Without it GNU as rounds the section up to the 32 KB
alignment of reset.s's trap table, and the file is 32 KB of which only the
first 768 bytes are code (a binutils without the option gives that file, with
the same code). gnu's q.bin is smaller than the sun one; comparing the sizes of
q.bin between the two paths means nothing.

What is checked instead is functional equivalence: matching offsets.h values,
symbol-level disassembly agreement for reset.s, and both toolchains booting to
the same banner.

offsets.h coverage
------------------
With the default generator offsets.h is byte-identical to the Sun reference:
all 1161 names, nothing supplemented. A few names do not resolve for either
tool (snet_info: offsets.in names it, no included header defines it); Sun's
stabs reports the same "Can't find snet_info".

The generator also reproduces Sun's rule that a struct with no member list
yields all its members (that is where the 90 HDNAME_* come from), which the
earlier genoffsets.py route could not do.

GCC 15 and later: C23
---------------------
GCC 15 defaults to C23. The hypervisor's sources predate it, and two things
change meaning: `false` (and `true`) are keywords, so a header that uses
`false` as an enumeration constant no longer compiles, and `f()` means
`f(void)`. Compile with -std=gnu17 instead. ./configure detects the need (the
four hypervisor sources are compiled as they are, then with the flag) and sets
GNU_CSTD; without configure:

    make GNU_CSTD=-std=gnu17

Without the flag hv_reset stops on `false` as an enumeration constant; with it
`make all` and the smoke test pass.
